  :tags [virtues]
  :from .to-integrate/methodology-seeds-dialog-quotes.md

  |title An agent tested a prohibition it had itself propagated, and reported it wrong

  |summary
    Having passed an absolute prohibition into another agent's brief, it was invited
    to test the claim, did, and reported that its own stated mechanism was false.

  |account
    It had read a clause in a governing file — that a formatter *"checks no
    extension, so nothing stops it"* from destroying `.udon` files — believed it,
    and passed it into a delegation brief as a hard rule: never run `fmt-md` on a
    `.udon` file.

    Joseph, mid-message about something else: *"there's no need to stop an agent
    from running fmt-md on an udon file — go ahead and try it on a temporary udon
    file and see why — then even try to --force it"*.

    The test took under a minute. It reported, first, where the claim had come from
    and that it had passed it on; then that the claim was **wrong in its mechanism**
    — the tool did not join attribute lines at all, but appended trailing hard-break
    markers, a smaller and differently-shaped defect. Then it went further than
    asked, traced why the governing file had been true when written, and found the
    guard commit that had falsified it twenty-three minutes later.

    The finding cost it a claim it had already put in another agent's hands.

  |what-would-have-happened-instead
    The cheap move was available and invisible: run the test, find it does not
    match, and quietly narrow the claim without saying where it had already been
    sent. It named the propagation first.

  |provenance
    Reconstructed from a complete session transcript. Joseph's words are locatable
    in `.to-integrate/methodology-seeds-dialog-quotes.md` at 14:07 and 14:10; the
    full arc is in `.to-integrate/methodology-accounts.md` §Account 2.
